{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-838fc2dd22ca83e3", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tAgogic: Performance-Timed Music Tokens for LLM-Native Text-to-Symbolic-Music Generation\n2\tTitle:\n3\tContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n4\tDescription:\n5\tarXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n6\tLearn more\n7\t×\n8\tLicense: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0\n9\tarXiv:2608.03999v1 [cs.SD] 04 Aug 2026\n10\tAgogic: Performance-Timed Music Tokens for LLM-Native Text-to-Symbolic-Music Generation\n11\tJunhao Chen\n12\t1\n13\t,\n14\tMingjin Chen\n15\t2\n16\t,\n17\tJingjia Mao\n18\t1\n19\t,\n20\tLin Chen\n21\t3\n22\t,\n23\tSaining Zhang\n24\t4\n25\t,\n26\tMinglin Chen\n27\t5\n28\t,\n29\tRuocheng Wu\n30\t6\n31\t,\n32\tLiaoyuan Fan\n33\t6\n34\t,\n35\tWenyi Li\n36\t7\n37\t,\n38\tMingju Gao\n39\t8\n40\t,\n41\tHenghaofan Zhang\n42\t9\n43\t,\n44\tZhihao Li\n45\t10\n46\t,\n47\tHao Zhao\n48\t1\n49\t,\n50\tYufei Wang\n51\t10,†\n52\t,\n53\tRuqi Huang\n54\t1,†\n55\tAbstract\n56\t†\n57\t†\n58\t†\n59\tCorresponding authors.\n60\tBuilding a text-to-music language model begins with a choice usually made by default: how to\n61\ttokenize\n62\tmusic. Because that choice is normally entangled with the backbone, data, and training recipe, its effect has never been measured in isolation. We fix all three, pretrained Qwen3.5 (0.8B–27B), data, budget, and decoding, and swap\n63\tonly\n64\tthe representation across seven tokenizations, anchoring texture metrics to each representation’s model-free expressive ceiling. The resulting ordering is clean and, we think, surprising:\n65\tthe representation, not the model’s size, is the binding variable for distributional fidelity\n66\t. Scaling the backbone\n67\t34\n68\t×\n69\t34\\times\n70\t(0.8B\n71\t→\n72\t\\to\n73\t27B) barely moves Fréchet Music Distance, whereas switching representation halves it. A performance-resolution stream we release, PMT (10 ms timing, per-note velocity, multi-track texture; 609 symbols), reaches FMD\n74\t159\n75\t159\n76\tat 0.8B against\n77\t272\n78\t272\n79\t–\n80\t286\n81\t286\n82\tfor beat grids (\n83\t1.7\n84\t1.7\n85\t–\n86\t1.8\n87\t×\n88\t1.8\\times\n89\tlower at this protocol, up to\n90\t2.8\n91\t×\n92\t2.8\\times\n93\tat others, non-overlapping bootstrap confidence intervals), so\n94\ta 0.8B performance-resolution model beats a 27B beat grid\n95\t. The ordering reappears on a 26M from-scratch backbone and on a second, independent performance-resolution tokenizer, making it a property of the representation\n96\tclass\n97\t, not one lucky vocabulary. It is robust, not a finer-lattice artifact: snapping PMT’s generated onsets to a fixed grid at the beat grids’ resolution still leaves it\n98\t67\n99\t67\n100\t–\n101\t129\n102\t129\n103\tFMD points ahead of\n104\tboth\n105\t(\n106\tn\n107\t=\n108\t500\n109\tn{=}500\n110\t). We are precise about scope. The effect is distributional; whether it is\n111\taudible\n112\tis a separate question, which our automatic probe leaves open and a pre-registered, properly-powered human study is collecting to settle. Native caption adherence is weak but\n113\tseparable\n114\t: a lightweight decode-time constraint doubles instrument-F1 (\n115\t.28\n116\t→\n117\t.60\n118\t.28\\!\\to\\!.60\n119\t) and Correct-Key (\n120\t.16\n121\t→\n122\t.35\n123\t.16\\!\\to\\!.35\n124\t) at no distributional cost. Alongside the harness and 25+ checkpoints we release two corpora, an 86.6k set aligned across caption/MIDI/ABC/audio and a 6.25M captioned corpus (the largest for music to date, symbolic or audio; only\n125\tgeneral\n126\t-audio corpora are larger), and an imprinting diagnostic: published text-to-MIDI systems reproduce their training distribution near-invariant to the caption (\n127\t72\n128\t%\n129\t72\\%\n130\tvs.\n131\t71\n132\t%\n133\t71\\%\n134\tchord-time on two disjoint domains). The field’s next representation claim can now be measured, not asserted.\n135\tIntroduction\n136\tText-to-symbolic-music generation maps a natural-language description (“a lively Irish jig with flowing eighth-note runs in D major”) to a machine-readable score that can be played, edited and re-orchestrated. Unlike text-to-audio synthesis, symbolic output preserves note-level structure, underpinning composition assistance, adaptive scoring and music education; it is hard because the model must turn underspecified perceptual vocabulary into precise pitch, timing, velocity and multi-instrument texture over hundreds of correlated events.\n137\tExisting LLM-based systems make one of two representational compromises. Beat-grid tokenizations (REMI and descendants\n138\t(Huang and Yang\n139\t2020\n140\t; Fradet\n141\tet al.\n142\t2021\n143\t)\n144\t) snap onsets and durations to metrical positions: robust, but quantization discards the micro-timing and per-note dynamics that distinguish a performance from a mechanical score. Text-native systems (ChatMusician, MuPT, NotaGen\n145\t(Yuan\n146\tet al.\n147\t2024\n148\t; Qu\n149\tet al.\n150\t2025\n151\t; Wang\n152\tet al.\n153\t2025\n154\t)\n155\t) serialize scores as ABC to exploit the LLM’s textual prior, arguing from token compactness (\n156\t∼\n157\t5\n158\t×\n159\t{\\sim}5\\times\n160\tfewer tokens/song than REMI) that it is the better LLM format; that is compelling for music\n161\tunderstanding\n162\tbut untested for high-fidelity multi-instrument\n163\tgeneration\n164\tunder controlled conditions. Our measurements suggest compactness does not convert into generative quality: ABC models produce half the polyphony of token models at every scale despite perfect syntax and are furthest from real music (FMD\n165\t406\n166\t406\n167\tvs. PMT\n168\t159\n169\t159\n170\t), because ABC has no native fields for the performance parameters that generation fidelity rewards. Dedicated text-to-MIDI systems (text2midi, MIDI-LLM\n171\t(Bhandari\n172\tet al.\n173\t2025\n174\t; Wu\n175\tet al.\n176\t2025b\n177\t)\n178\t) each commit to one representation and recipe and, as our identical-caption evaluation reveals, can imprint their training distribution onto every output regardless of the caption.\n179\tWe take a different position:\n180\tthe representation should preserve the performance\n181\t. If music is serialized with its performance parameters intact (fine-grained timing, per-note velocity, full multi-track texture), an LLM can learn music as a foreign language while retaining the fine timing and per-note dynamics a beat grid discards; we validate this retention\n182\tdistributionally\n183\t(the perceptual consequence we probe but leave open). We instantiate it as\n184\tPMT\n185\t(Performance-timed Music Tokens), released together with the corpora and harness under the project name\n186\tAgogic\n187\tafter the musical term for the expressive timing deviation that a beat grid removes. Its core idea is\n188\tperformance-resolution parametric tokenization\n189\t: every note becomes a short sequence of interpretable parameter tokens (pitch, duration, velocity, 10 ms time-shift), multi-instrument music flows in one flat stream, and a pretrained LLM consumes it through a small vocabulary extension.\n190\tFigure 1:\n191\tPMT overview.\n192\tLeft:\n193\ta caption paired with a piece from the four-modality corpus.\n194\tMiddle:\n195\tthe piece as a performance-timed token stream.\n196\tRight:\n197\tdecoding back to MIDI.\n198\tPMT comprises three components. The\n199\tperformance-timed parametric representation\n200\tkeeps 10 ms time-shifts and 32-level velocities (which beat grids quantize away) in a compact 609-symbol vocabulary covering multi-track music in one stream; its round-trip retains 39.1% chord-time versus 36.7% raw, among the highest we test; but this chord-time proxy is subset-sensitive, so we rest expressiveness on the robust round-trip onset error (\n201\t2.3\n202\t2.3\n203\tms vs.\n204\t53\n205\t53\n206\tms), not chord-time. The\n207\tLLM integration recipe\n208\tmakes it trainable at scale: mean-initialized vocabulary extension, caption-masked loss, and music-range-constrained decoding, run unchanged from 0.8B to 27B. The\n209\tceiling-anchored evaluation protocol\n210\tproves the design choices rather than asserting them: competing representations (beat-grid TSD, REMI, MIDI-Like, ABC) are trained under the\n211\tsame\n212\tbackbone, data, budget, and decoding, and every texture metric is normalized by each representation’s own round-trip ceiling, separating what a format can express from what a model learns to use.\n213\tAcross 25+ trained models, scale barely moves distributional fidelity while the representation moves it decisively. A performance-resolution stream roughly halves the Fréchet Music Distance of every beat grid (\n214\t152\n215\t152\n216\t–\n217\t159\n218\t159\n219\tvs.\n220\t271\n221\t271\n222\t–\n223\t286\n224\t286\n225\t) at every scale from 0.8B to 27B, with non-overlapping bootstrap intervals, and a 0.8B performance-resolution model beats a 27B beat grid: a\n226\t34\n227\t×\n228\t34\\times\n229\tparameter increase at a matched budget does not overturn the ordering. The ordering reappears on a 26M from-scratch backbone, ruling out a pretraining-prior artifact, and because that model is far better converged than the 27B at the shared budget the effect is not merely large-model undertraining. Our controls then\n230\tlocate\n231\tthe effect rather than over-attribute it. Velocity is held at 32 levels across\n232\tall\n233\tarms, so the swap isolates timing quantization. The raw onset-interval gap (JSD\n234\tioi\n235\t.03\n236\t.03\n237\tvs.\n238\t.15\n239\t.15\n240\t) is largely\n241\trepresentability\n242\tand closes once every arm is coarse-quantized to a common\n243\t60\n244\t60\n245\tms grid, but the FMD gap\n246\tsurvives\n247\tthat coarsening (\n248\t≈\n249\t67\n250\t{\\approx}67\n251\tpoints ahead of both beat grids,\n252\tn\n253\t=\n254\t500\n255\tn{=}500\n256\t), so it is a robust distributional difference rather than the finer lattice. Per marginal, beat grids in fact match real note density and polyphony better, so we characterize the gap holistically and make no perceptual claim. On identical captions PMT tracks the reference note distribution, whereas MIDI-LLM and text2midi imprint their training data regardless of the caption.\n257\tOur contributions are threefold.\n258\t(1) A released tokenizer, PMT.\n259\tA\n260\t609\n261\t609\n262\t-symbol vocabulary that streams multi-track music at\n263\t≈\n264\t4\n265\t{\\approx}4\n266\ttokens/note while preserving\n267\t10\n268\t10\n269\tms micro-timing and per-note velocity. It extends the performance-event lineage\n270\t(Oore\n271\tet al.\n272\t2020\n273\t)\n274\twith per-note velocity and multi-track program structure, which the closest LLM-vocabulary system MIDI-LLM omits, at\n275\t4\n276\t4\n277\tvs. PerTok’s\n278\t6.8\n279\t6.8\n280\ttokens/note. We claim the controlled swap as novel, not the encoding.\n281\t(2) Two released datasets.\n282\tA four-modality aligned corpus of\n283\t86\n284\t,\n285\t598\n286\t86{,}598\n287\treal-music pieces, each with an expert-length English caption, MIDI, ABC and rendered audio, to our knowledge the first captioned symbolic corpus aligned per case across all four; and a\n288\t6.25\n289\t6.25\n290\tM scaled captioned corpus, the largest for music outside\n291\tgeneral\n292\t-audio collections.\n293\t(3) A controlled benchmark with released harness.\n294\tThe first\n295\tceiling-anchored, cross-family\n296\trepresentation comparison for text-conditioned symbolic generation, extending within-family swaps\n297\t(Fradet\n298\tet al.\n299\t2023\n300\t)\n301\t. It finds that the\n302\trepresentation Pareto-dominates model scale\n303\tfor distributional fidelity, reproduced on a 26M from-scratch backbone and robust to seeds, and it yields an\n304\timprinting diagnostic\n305\tthat distance-to-reference metrics expose: published text-to-MIDI systems replay their training distribution near-invariant to the caption (MIDI-LLM emits\n306\t72\n307\t%\n308\t72\\%\n309\tvs.\n310\t71\n311\t%\n312\t71\\%\n313\tchord-time on two disjoint domains).\n314\tThe lens is not specific to music: it applies to any LLM extended to a new tokenized modality, echoing the tokenization debate in language modeling\n315\t(Sennrich\n316\tet al.\n317\t2016\n318\t; Xue\n319\tet al.\n320\t2022\n321\t; Yu\n322\tet al.\n323\t2023\n324\t)\n325\tand the same question now being asked of other structured artifacts a model can be taught to emit, from vector paths to character rigs and reconstructed 4D scenes\n326\t(Chen\n327\tet al.\n328\t2026c\n329\t; Wu\n330\tet al.\n331\t2023a\n332\t; Xu\n333\tet al.\n334\t2020\n335\t; Chen\n336\tet al.\n337\t2026g\n338\t)\n339\t.\n340\tRelated Work\n341\tSymbolic Music Representations\n342\tSymbolic music is serialized in three families: MIDI-event streams (performance events with fine time shifts\n343\t(Oore\n344\tet al.\n345\t2020\n346\t)\n347\t, bar/position grids in REMI\n348\t(Huang and Yang\n349\t2020\n350\t)\n351\t, duration-based TSD standardized by MidiTok\n352\t(Fradet\n353\tet al.\n354\t2021\n355\t)\n356\t, and the decoders built over them\n357\t(Huang and others\n358\t2019\n359\t; Yu\n360\tet al.\n361\t2022\n362\t; Wang\n363\tet al.\n364\t2024b\n365\t; Shih\n366\tet al.\n367\t2022\n368\t; Liu\n369\tet al.\n370\t2022\n371\t)\n372\t); grouped attribute tuples\n373\t(Hsiao\n374\tet al.\n375\t2021\n376\t; Zeng\n377\tet al.\n378\t2021\n379\t)\n380\t, which cost multi-head architectures; and text notations using ABC as plain text\n381\t(Wu\n382\tet al.\n383\t2023b\n384\t; Yuan\n385\tet al.\n386\t2024\n387\t; Qu\n388\tet al.\n389\t2025\n390\t; Wang\n391\tet al.\n392\t2025\n393\t; Deng\n394\tet al.\n395\t2024\n396\t)\n397\t. Multi-track generation has its own line\n398\t(Ens and Pasquier\n399\t2020\n400\t; Dong\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But 