exture; 609 symbols), reaches FMD\n159\n159\nat 0.8B against\n272\n272\n–\n286\n286\nfor beat grids (\n1.7\n1.7\n–\n1.8\n×\n1.8\\times\nlower at this protocol, up to\n2.8\n×\n2.8\\times\nat others, non-overlapping bootstrap confidence intervals), so\na 0.8B performance-resolution model beats a 27B beat grid\n. The ordering reappears on a 26M from-scratch backbone and on a second, independent performance-resolution tokenizer, making it a property of the representation\nclass\n, not one lucky vocabulary. It is robust, not a finer-lattice artifact: snapping PMT’s generated onsets to a fixed grid at the beat grids’ resolution still leaves it\n67\n67\n–\n129\n129\nFMD points ahead of\nboth\n(\nn\n=\n500\nn{=}500\n). We are precise about scope. The effect is distributional; whether it is\naudible\nis a separate question, which our automatic probe leaves open and a pre-registered, properly-powered human study is collecting to settle. Native caption adherence is weak but\nseparable\n: a lightweight decode-time constraint doubles instrument-F1 (\n.28\n→\n.60\n.28\\!\\to\\!.60\n) and Correct-Key (\n.16\n→\n.35\n.16\\!\\to\\!.35\n) at no distributional cost. Alongside the harness and 25+ checkpoints we release two corpora, an 86.6k set aligned across caption/MIDI/ABC/audio and a 6.25M captioned corpus (the largest for music to date, symbolic or audio; only\ngeneral\n-audio corpora are larger), and an imprinting diagnostic: published text-to-MIDI systems reproduce their training distribution near-invariant to the caption (\n72\n%\n72\\%\nvs.\n71\n%\n71\\%\nchord-time on two disjoint domains). The field’s next representation claim can now be measured, not asserted.\nIntroduction\nText-to-symbolic-music generation maps a natural-language description (“a lively Irish jig with flowing eighth-note runs in D major”) to a machine-readable score that can be played, edited and re-orchestrated. Unlike text-to-audio synthesis, symbolic output preserves note-level structure, underpinning composition assistance, adaptive scoring and music education; it is hard because the model must turn underspecified perceptual vocabulary into precise pitch, timing, velocity and multi-instrument texture over hundreds of correlated events.\nExisting LLM-based systems make one of two representational compromises. Beat-grid tokenizations (REMI and descendants\n(Huang and Yang\n2020\n; Fradet\net al.\n2021\n)\n) snap onsets and durations to metrical positions: robust, but quantization discards the micro-timing and per-note dynamics that distinguish a performance from a mechanical score. Text-native systems (ChatMusician, MuPT, NotaGen\n(Yuan\net al.\n2024\n; Qu\net al.\n2025\n; Wang\net al.\n2025\n)\n) serialize scores as ABC to exploit the LLM’s textual prior, arguing from token compactness (\n∼\n5\n×\n{\\sim}5\\times\nfewer tokens/song than REMI) that it is the better LLM format; that is compelling for music\nunderstanding\nbut untested for high-fidelity multi-instrument\ngeneration\nunder controlled conditions. Our measurements suggest compactness does not convert into generative quality: ABC models produce half the polyphony of token models at every scale despite perfect syntax and are furthest from real music (FMD\n406\n406\nvs. PMT\n159\n159\n), because ABC has no native fields for the performance parameters that generation fidelity rewards. Dedicated text-to-MIDI systems (text2midi, MIDI-LLM\n(Bhandari\net al.\n2025\n; Wu\net al.\n2025b\n)\n) each commit to one representation and recipe and, as our identical-caption evaluation reveals, can imprint their training distribution onto every output regardless of the caption.\nWe take a different position:\nthe representation should preserve the performance\n. If music is serialized with its performance parameters intact (fine-grained timing, per-note velocity, full multi-track texture), an LLM can learn music as a foreign language while retaining the fine timing and per-note dynamics a beat grid discards; we validate this retention\ndistributionally\n(the perceptual consequence we probe but leave open). We instantiate it as\nPMT\n(Performance-timed Music Tokens), released together with the corpora and harness under the project name\nAgogic\nafter the musical term for the expressive timing deviation that a beat grid removes. Its core idea is\nperformance-resolution parametric tokenization\n: every note becomes a short sequence of interpretable parameter tokens (pitch, duration, velocity, 10 ms time-shift), multi-instrument music flows in one flat stream, and a pretrained LLM consumes it through a small vocabulary extension.\nFigure 1:\nPMT overview.\nLeft:\na caption paired with a piece from the four-modality corpus.\nMiddle:\nthe piece as a performance-timed token stream.\nRight:\ndecoding back to MIDI.\nPMT comprises three components. The\nperformance-timed parametric representation\nkeeps 10 ms time-shifts and 32-level velocities (which beat grids quantize away) in a compact 609-symbol vocabulary covering multi-track music in one stream; its round-trip retains 39.1% chord-time versus 36.7% raw, among the highest we test; but this chord-time proxy is subset-sensitive, so we rest expressiveness on the robust round-trip onset error (\n2.3\n2.3\nms vs.\n53\n53\nms), not chord-time. The\nLLM integration recipe\nmakes it trainable at scale: mean-initialized vocabulary extension, caption-masked loss, and music-range-constrained decoding, run unchanged from 0.8B to 27B. The\nceiling-anchored evaluation protocol\nproves the design choices rather than asserting them: competing representations (beat-grid TSD, REMI, MIDI-Like, ABC) are trained under the\nsame\nbackbone, data, budget, and decoding, and every texture metric is normalized by each representation’s own round-trip ceiling, separating what a format can express from what a model learns to use.\nAcross 25+ trained models, scale barely moves distributional fidelity while the representation moves it decisively. A performance-resolution stream roughly halves the Fréchet Music Distance of every beat grid (\n152\n152\n–\n159\n159\nvs.\n271\n271\n–\n286\n286\n) at every scale from 0.8B to 27B, with non-overlapping bootstrap intervals, and a 0.8B performance-resolution model beats a 27B beat grid: a\n34\n×\n34\\times\nparameter increase at a matched budget does not overturn the ordering. The ordering reappears on a 26M from-scratch backbone, ruling out a pretraining-prior artifact, and because that model is far better converged than the 27B at the shared budget the effect is not merely large-model undertraining. Our controls then\nlocate\nthe effect rather than over-attribute it. Velocity is held at 32 levels across\nall\narms, so the swap isolates timing quantization. The raw onset-interval gap (JSD\nioi\n.03\n.03\nvs.\n.15\n.15\n) is largely\nrepresentability\nand closes once every arm is coarse-quantized to a common\n60\n60\nms grid, but the FMD gap\nsurvives\nthat coarsening (\n≈\n67\n{\\approx}67\npoints ahead of both beat grids,\nn\n=\n500\nn{=}500\n), so it is a robust distributional difference rather than the finer lattice. Per marginal, beat grids in fact match real note density and polyphony better, so we characterize the gap holistically and make no perceptual claim. On identical captions PMT tracks the reference note distribution, whereas MIDI-LLM and text2midi imprint their training data regardless of the caption.\nOur contributions are threefold.\n(1) A released tokenizer, PMT.\nA\n609\n609\n-symbol vocabulary that streams multi-track music at\n≈\n4\n{\\approx}4\ntokens/note while preserving\n10\n10\nms micro-timing and per-note velocity. It extends the performance-event lineage\n(Oore\net al.\n2020\n)\nwith per-note velocity and multi-track program structure, which the closest LLM-vocabulary system MIDI-LLM omits, at\n4\n4\nvs. PerTok’s\n6.8\n6.8\ntokens/note. We claim the controlled swap as novel, not the encoding.\n(2) Two released datasets.\nA four-modality aligned corpus of\n86\n,\n598\n86{,}598\nreal-music pieces, each with an expert-length English caption, MIDI, ABC and rendered audio, to our knowledge the first captioned symbolic corpus aligned per case across all four; and a\n6.25\n6.25\nM scaled captioned corpus, the largest for music outside\ngeneral\n-audio collections.\n(3) A controlled benchmark with released harness.\nThe first\nceiling-anchored, cross-family\nrepresentation comparison for text-conditioned symbolic generation, extending within-family swaps\n(Fradet\net al.\n2023\n)\n. It finds that the\nrepresentation Pareto-dominates model scale\nfor distributional fidelity, reproduced on a 26M from-scratch backbone and robust to seeds, and it yields an\nimprinting diagnostic\nthat distance-to-reference metrics expose: published text-to-MIDI systems replay their training distribution near-invariant to the caption (MIDI-LLM emits\n72\n%\n72\\%\nvs.\n71\n%\n71\\%\nchord-time on two disjoint domains).\nThe lens is not specific to music: it applies to any LLM extended to a new tokenized modality, echoing the tokenization debate in language modeling\n(Sennrich\net al.\n2016\n; Xue\net al.\n2022\n; Yu\net al.\n2023\n)\nand the same question now being asked of other structured artifacts a model can be taught to emit, from vector paths to character rigs and reconstructed 4D scenes\n(Chen\net al.\n2026c\n; Wu\net al.\n2023a\n; Xu\net al.\n2020\n; Chen\net al.\n2026g\n)\n.\nRelated Work\nSymbolic Music Representations\nSymbolic music is serialized in three families: MIDI-event streams (performance events with fine time shifts\n(Oore\net al.\n2020\n)\n, bar/position grids in REMI\n(Huang and Yang\n2020\n)\n, duration-based TSD standardized by MidiTok\n(Fradet\net al.\n2021\n)\n, and the decoders built over them\n(Huang and others\n2019\n; Yu\net al.\n2022\n; Wang\net al.\n2024b\n; Shih\net al.\n2022\n; Liu\net al.\n2022\n)\n); grouped attribute tuples\n(Hsiao\net al.\n2021\n; Zeng\net al.\n2021\n)\n, which cost multi-head architectures; and text notations using ABC as plain text\n(Wu\net al.\n2023b\n; Yuan\net al.\n2024\n; Qu\net al.\n2025\n; Wang\net al.\n2025\n; Deng\net al.\n2024\n)\n. Multi-track generation has its own line\n(Ens and Pasquier\n2020\n; Dong", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 12535}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785924125688", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-907ccfbd80f5c74a", "name": "Read", "input": {"file_path": "/tmp/agogic_text.txt", "offset": 400, "limit": 500}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fa787f0e-de86-4690-a3fa-8cf13e967722", "uuid": "bf68b682-e1ec-43d5-bbc0-7237d0a08aa9"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-907ccfbd80f5c74a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\t; Dong\n401\tet al.\n402\t2023\n403\t; von Rütte\n404\tet al.\n405\t2023\n406\t; Malandro\n407\t2024\n408\t; Xu\n409\tet al.\n410\t2020\n411\t)\n412\t; pressure to make the encoding itself cheap recurs in other sequence domains\n413\t(Chen\n414\tet al.\n415\t2023b\n416\t; Xu\n417\tet al.\n418\t2026b\n419\t; Chen\n420\tet al.\n421\t2026h\n422\t)\n423\t, as does full-song and long-sequence modelling\n424\t(Chen\n425\tet al.\n426\t2025c\n427\t; Dai\n428\tet al.\n429\t2026\n430\t)\n431\t, and the same parameterise-or-render choice recurs for character rigs and object skeletons\n432\t(Sun\n433\tet al.\n434\t2025\n435\t; Xu\n436\tet al.\n437\t2020\n438\t; Sun\n439\tet al.\n440\t2026\n441\t)\n442\t, for reconstructed scenes and 3D assets, where dense fields stand apart from structured, editable output\n443\t(Poole\n444\tet al.\n445\t2023\n446\t)\n447\t, for interaction video\n448\t(Owens\n449\tet al.\n450\t2016\n451\t; Chen\n452\tet al.\n453\t2026i\n454\t)\n455\t, for vector graphics\n456\t(Wu\n457\tet al.\n458\t2023a\n459\t; Xing\n460\tet al.\n461\t2024\n462\t)\n463\t, and for generated avatars, where geometry and texture are produced apart\n464\t(Chen\n465\tet al.\n466\t2026j\n467\t)\n468\t; PMT instead flattens all tracks into one performance-timed stream consumable by an off-the-shelf single-softmax LLM, descending from the performance-event lineage of\n469\tOore\n470\tet al.\n471\t(\n472\t2020\n473\t)\n474\t. Learned discrete codes are a third option: MuseTok quantizes bar-wise segments with an RQ-VAE\n475\t(Huang\n476\tet al.\n477\t2026\n478\t)\n479\t, as discrete latent vocabularies do for other structured artifacts\n480\t(Weng\n481\tet al.\n482\t2026a\n483\t)\n484\t, and Pianoroll-Event mixes piano-roll frames with event tokens for encoding efficiency\n485\t(Qian\n486\tet al.\n487\t2026a\n488\t)\n489\t; window-level latents\n490\t(Yao and Feng\n491\t2026\n492\t)\n493\t, delay-based token scheduling\n494\t(Wang\n495\tet al.\n496\t2026\n497\t)\n498\tand 2D spectrogram grids\n499\t(Cheng\n500\tet al.\n501\t2026\n502\t)\n503\tcontinue to widen the design space; both target the encoding itself, whereas we hold the encoding fixed per arm and compare families. Prior comparisons stay within one family\n504\t(Fradet\n505\tet al.\n506\t2023\n507\t; Wang\n508\tet al.\n509\t2024a\n510\t; Qian\n511\tet al.\n512\t2026b\n513\t)\n514\t, and the editable-parameterisation goal is shared beyond music, from editable illustrations and character rigs to part-level 3D editing and engine-native scene reconstruction\n515\t(Chen\n516\tet al.\n517\t2026f\n518\t; Xu\n519\tet al.\n520\t2020\n521\t; Weng\n522\tet al.\n523\t2026b\n524\t; Poole\n525\tet al.\n526\t2023\n527\t; Chen\n528\tet al.\n529\t2026b\n530\t)\n531\tor benchmark representations for\n532\tclassification and understanding\n533\t(Zhang\n534\tet al.\n535\t2023b\n536\t; Strepetov and Kovalev\n537\t2025\n538\t)\n539\trather than the controlled, cross-family, text-conditioned\n540\tgeneration\n541\twe study.\n542\tText-Conditioned Symbolic Music Generation\n543\tText-to-MIDI systems pair a caption encoder with a music decoder: text2midi couples FLAN-T5 with a REMI decoder\n544\t(Bhandari\n545\tet al.\n546\t2025\n547\t)\n548\t; MIDI-LLM extends Llama-3.2’s vocabulary with MIDI events\n549\t(Wu\n550\tet al.\n551\t2025b\n552\t)\n553\t; MuseCoco routes free text through musical attributes\n554\t(Lu\n555\tet al.\n556\t2023\n557\t)\n558\t, and attribute- or emotion-conditioned lines control the same axes without free text\n559\t(Kang\n560\tet al.\n561\t2023\n562\t; Huang\n563\tet al.\n564\t2024\n565\t)\n566\t; the ABC line reaches text conditioning by instruction tuning or metadata prompts\n567\t(Wu and Sun\n568\t2023\n569\t; Lu\n570\tet al.\n571\t2023\n572\t)\n573\t; a further route keeps the artifact in a language the model already writes and lets an interpreter realise it, whether code, vector paths or an edit instruction\n574\t(Chen\n575\tet al.\n576\t2026e\n577\t; Wu\n578\tet al.\n579\t2023a\n580\t; Chen\n581\tet al.\n582\t2025a\n583\t; Xing\n584\tet al.\n585\t2024\n586\t; Chen\n587\tet al.\n588\t2026d\n589\t; Lu\n590\tet al.\n591\t2023\n592\t; Chen\n593\tet al.\n594\t2023a\n595\t)\n596\t. Each entangles its representation with its data and recipe, so published numbers cannot isolate either. PMT is an LLM-vocabulary-extension method like MIDI-LLM, which likewise encodes fine (\n597\t10\n598\t10\n599\tms) arrival time\n600\t(Thickstun\n601\tet al.\n602\t2023\n603\t)\n604\t, but also preserves per-note velocity (which MIDI-LLM omits) and multi-track program structure and, more importantly, differs in\n605\tvalidation\n606\t: we hold backbone, data and budget fixed and swap only the representation, and running MIDI-LLM through our harness on identical captions reveals that it imprints its training distribution (\n607\t72\n608\t%\n609\t72\\%\n610\tchord-time against a\n611\t36.7\n612\t%\n613\t36.7\\%\n614\treference) where PMT matches within 1pp. Very recent systems continue both lines, symbolic\n615\t(Su\n616\tet al.\n617\t2026\n618\t; Bhandari\n619\tet al.\n620\t2026\n621\t)\n622\tand audio\n623\t(Copet\n624\tet al.\n625\t2023\n626\t; Liu\n627\tet al.\n628\t2024a\n629\t; Evans\n630\tet al.\n631\t2025\n632\t; Yuan\n633\tet al.\n634\t2026\n635\t; Xu\n636\tet al.\n637\t2026a\n638\t; Google DeepMind Magenta Team\n639\t2026\n640\t)\n641\tbuilt on neural audio codecs and audio language models\n642\t(Zeghidour\n643\tet al.\n644\t2021\n645\t; Défossez\n646\tet al.\n647\t2023\n648\t; Dhariwal\n649\tet al.\n650\t2020\n651\t; Kreuk\n652\tet al.\n653\t2023\n654\t)\n655\t; video generation is the instructive counter-case, and it separates two things that are easily conflated. Human-centric video models emit pixels and no audio at all\n656\t(Chen\n657\tet al.\n658\t2026a\n659\t; Owens\n660\tet al.\n661\t2016\n662\t; Miao\n663\tet al.\n664\t2026\n665\t)\n666\t, and the models that do emit sound alongside video produce a joint waveform\n667\t(HaCohen\n668\tet al.\n669\t2026\n670\t; Team\n671\tet al.\n672\t2026\n673\t)\n674\t, as does the video-to-audio line before them\n675\t(Luo\n676\tet al.\n677\t2023\n678\t; Gan\n679\tet al.\n680\t2020\n681\t; Zhang\n682\tet al.\n683\t2026\n684\t)\n685\t. That is\n686\tsound\n687\tgeneration, which is not music generation, and in either case a waveform offers no handle on key, chord, instrument or note: what a model can be asked to control is fixed by what it is asked to emit; a parallel line builds music- and audio-\n688\tunderstanding\n689\tmodels\n690\t(Gardner\n691\tet al.\n692\t2024\n693\t; Liu\n694\tet al.\n695\t2024b\n696\t; Chu\n697\tet al.\n698\t2023\n699\t; Li\n700\tet al.\n701\t2024\n702\t; Ye\n703\tet al.\n704\t2024\n705\t)\n706\t, one of which (Qwen3-Omni) supplies our captions. Appendix\n707\tA\n708\ttreats the closest of these in full.\n709\tEvaluation of Symbolic Music Generation\n710\tEvaluation here is fragmented: MidiCaps supplies the de-facto text–MIDI dataset\n711\t(Melechovsky\n712\tet al.\n713\t2024b\n714\t)\n715\twithout a protocol; papers mix perplexity, feature histograms\n716\t(Yang and Lerch\n717\t2020\n718\t)\n719\tand rendered-audio scores\n720\t(Kilgour\n721\tet al.\n722\t2019\n723\t)\n724\t; FMD contributes a symbolic Fréchet distance over CLaMP-2\n725\t(Retkowski\n726\tet al.\n727\t2024\n728\t)\n729\tand CLaMP-3 a symbolic-native alignment score\n730\t(Wu\n731\tet al.\n732\t2025a\n733\t)\n734\t; surveys document the absence of standardized benchmarks\n735\t(Xiong\n736\tet al.\n737\t2023\n738\t; Ma\n739\tet al.\n740\t2024\n741\t)\n742\t; benchmarks for other structured outputs face the same problem\n743\t(Guo\n744\tet al.\n745\t2025\n746\t; Fradet\n747\tet al.\n748\t2023\n749\t; Zhang\n750\tet al.\n751\t2023a\n752\t; Qian\n753\tet al.\n754\t2026b\n755\t; Chen\n756\tet al.\n757\t2025b\n758\t)\n759\t. Two pitfalls matter for method papers: per-token loss is incomparable across vocabularies, and raw texture statistics ignore that encodings differ in what they can express at all. Our ceiling-anchored protocol addresses both.\n760\tMethod\n761\tOverview\n762\tProblem definition.\n763\tGiven a natural-language description\n764\tc\n765\tc\n766\t, generate a symbolic score\n767\ty\n768\ty\n769\t, notes with pitch, onset, duration, velocity and instrument, that is well-formed (decodable and renderable), matches the description, and matches the statistical profile of real music. A representation\n770\tR\n771\tR\n772\tserializes\n773\ty\n774\ty\n775\tinto a token sequence with decoder\n776\tR\n777\t−\n778\t1\n779\tR^{-1}\n780\t; a text-to-music method is a choice of\n781\tR\n782\tR\n783\tplus a procedure for training\n784\tp\n785\tθ\n786\t​\n787\t(\n788\tR\n789\t​\n790\t(\n791\ty\n792\t)\n793\t|\n794\tc\n795\t)\n796\tp_{\\theta}(R(y)\\,|\\,c)\n797\t. PMT’s pipeline (Figure\n798\t1\n799\t) has three interlocking components: the\n800\trepresentation\n801\tencodes each piece into a flat music-token stream, the\n802\tintegration recipe\n803\tappends that stream to the caption and fine-tunes a pretrained backbone with the caption masked, and the\n804\tevaluation protocol\n805\tvalidates every design choice by training competing representations under the identical recipe. The recipe holds everything but the representation fixed, which is what lets the protocol attribute quality differences to the representation.\n806\tFigure 2:\n807\tOne excerpt through the encoding.\n808\tTop:\n809\tthe MIDI events as the file stores them.\n810\tBottom:\n811\tthe PMT token stream for the same notes.\n812\tComponent 1: Performance-Timed Parametric Representation\n813\tPMT serializes each note as an interpretable parameter tuple; Figure\n814\t2\n815\twalks one excerpt through it, a four-note chord on one track followed by a single onset advance, from the file’s own\n816\tnote_on\n817\tevents to the token stream and back. The vocabulary comprises\n818\tPITCH\n819\t(128),\n820\tDUR\n821\t(200 bins at 10 ms, covering 10–2000 ms),\n822\tVEL\n823\t(32 levels),\n824\tTSHIFT\n825\t(100 bins at 10 ms; gaps beyond 1 s emit repeated shifts),\n826\tTRACK\n827\t(16), and\n828\tPROG\n829\t(129) plus four special tokens, 609 symbols total. Small named vocabularies of this kind are how other structured artifacts are made LLM-writable\n830\t(Wu\n831\tet al.\n832\t2023a\n833\t; Xing\n834\tet al.\n835\t2024\n836\t)\n837\t, and naming the parts is what makes an output editable after the fact\n838\t(Poole\n839\tet al.\n840\t2023\n841\t; Xu\n842\tet al.\n843\t2020\n844\t)\n845\t,\n846\t≈\n847\t\\approx\n848\t4.0 tokens per note. Writing\n849\t[\n850\ta\n851\t,\n852\tb\n853\t)\n854\t[a,b)\n855\tfor a family’s integer index range and\n856\t𝒮\n857\t\\mathcal{S}\n858\tfor the four special tokens, the music vocabulary is the disjoint union of the six parameter families,\n859\t𝒱\n860\tmusic\n861\t=\n862\t\\displaystyle\\mathcal{V}_{\\text{music}}=\n863\tTRACK\n864\t[\n865\t0\n866\t,\n867\t16\n868\t)\n869\t⊔\n870\tPROG\n871\t[\n872\t0\n873\t,\n874\t129\n875\t)\n876\t⊔\n877\tPITCH\n878\t[\n879\t0\n880\t,\n881\t128\n882\t)\n883\t\\displaystyle\\ \\texttt{TRACK}_{[0,16)}\\sqcup\\texttt{PROG}_{[0,129)}\\sqcup\\texttt{PITCH}_{[0,128)}\n884\t⊔\n885\tDUR\n886\t[\n887\t1\n888\t,\n889\t201\n890\t)\n891\t⊔\n892\tVEL\n893\t[\n894\t0\n895\t,\n896\t32\n897\t)\n898\t⊔\n899\tTSHIFT\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fa787f0e-de86-4690-a3fa-8cf13e967722", "uuid": "185eb73d-6554-47a1-8e08-87e4502ce9ee", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T10:02:07.825Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/agogic_text.txt", "content": "; Dong\net al.\n2023\n; von Rütte\net al.\n2023\n; Malandro\n2024\n; Xu\net al.\n2020\n)\n; pressure to make the encoding itself cheap recurs in other sequence domains\n(Chen\net al.\n2023b\n; Xu\net al.\n2026b\n; Chen\net al.\n2026h\n)\n, as does full-song and long-sequence modelling\n(Chen\net al.\n2025c\n; Dai\net al.\n2026\n)\n, and the same parameterise-or-render choice recurs for character rigs and object skeletons\n(Sun\net al.\n2025\n; Xu\net al.\n2020\n; Sun\net al.\n2026\n)\n, for reconstructed scenes and 3D assets, where dense fields stand apart from structured, editable output\n(Poole\net al.\n2023\n)\n, for interaction video\n(Owens\net al.\n2016\n; Chen\net al.\n2026i\n)\n, for vector graphics\n(Wu\net al.\n2023a\n; Xing\net al.\n2024\n)\n, and for generated avatars, where geometry and texture are produced apart\n(Chen\net al.\n2026j\n)\n; PMT instead flattens all tracks into one performance-timed stream consumable by an off-the-shelf single-softmax LLM, descending from the performance-event lineage of\nOore\net al.\n(\n2020\n)\n. Learned discrete codes are a third option: MuseTok quantizes bar-wise segments with an RQ-VAE\n(Huang\net al.\n2026\n)\n, as discrete latent vocabularies do for other structured artifacts\n(Weng\net al.\n2026a\n)\n, and Pianoroll-Event mixes piano-roll frames with event tokens for encoding efficiency\n(Qian\net al.\n2026a\n)\n; window-level latents\n(Yao and Feng\n2026\n)\n, delay-based token scheduling\n(Wang\net al.\n2026\n)\nand 2D spectrogram grids\n(Cheng\net al.\n2026\n)\ncontinue to widen the design space; both target the encoding itself, whereas we hold the encoding fixed per arm and compare families. Prior comparisons stay within one family\n(Fradet\net al.\n2023\n; Wang\net al.\n2024a\n; Qian\net al.\n2026b\n)\n, and the editable-parameterisation goal is shared beyond music, from editable illustrations and character rigs to part-level 3D editing and engine-native scene reconstruction\n(Chen\net al.\n2026f\n; Xu\net al.\n2020\n; Weng\net al.\n2026b\n; Poole\net al.\n2023\n; Chen\net al.\n2026b\n)\nor benchmark representations for\nclassification and understanding\n(Zhang\net al.\n2023b\n; Strepetov and Kovalev\n2025\n)\nrather than the controlled, cross-family, text-conditioned\ngeneration\nwe study.\nText-Conditioned Symbolic Music Generation\nText-to-MIDI systems pair a caption encoder with a music decoder: text2midi couples FLAN-T5 with a REMI decoder\n(Bhandari\net al.\n2025\n)\n; MIDI-LLM extends Llama-3.2’s vocabulary with MIDI events\n(Wu\net al.\n2025b\n)\n; MuseCoco routes free text through musical attributes\n(Lu\net al.\n2023\n)\n, and attribute- or emotion-conditioned lines control the same axes without free text\n(Kang\net al.\n2023\n; Huang\net al.\n2024\n)\n; the ABC line reaches text conditioning by instruction tuning or metadata prompts\n(Wu and Sun\n2023\n; Lu\net al.\n2023\n)\n; a further route keeps the artifact in a language the model already writes and lets an interpreter realise it, whether code, vector paths or an edit instruction\n(Chen\net al.\n2026e\n; Wu\net al.\n2023a\n; Chen\net al.\n2025a\n; Xing\net al.\n2024\n; Chen\net al.\n2026d\n; Lu\net al.\n2023\n; Chen\net al.\n2023a\n)\n. Each entangles its representation with its data and recipe, so published numbers cannot isolate either. PMT is an LLM-vocabulary-extension method like MIDI-LLM, which likewise encodes fine (\n10\n10\nms) arrival time\n(Thickstun\net al.\n2023\n)\n, but also preserves per-note velocity (which MIDI-LLM omits) and multi-track program structure and, more importantly, differs in\nvalidation\n: we hold backbone, data and budget fixed and swap only the representation, and running MIDI-LLM through our harness on identical captions reveals that it imprints its training distribution (\n72\n%\n72\\%\nchord-time against a\n36.7\n%\n36.7\\%\nreference) where PMT matches within 1pp. Very recent systems continue both lines, symbolic\n(Su\net al.\n2026\n; Bhandari\net al.\n2026\n)\nand audio\n(Copet\net al.\n2023\n; Liu\net al.\n2024a\n; Evans\net al.\n2025\n; Yuan\net al.\n2026\n; Xu\net al.\n2026a\n; Google DeepMind Magenta Team\n2026\n)\nbuilt on neural audio codecs and audio language models\n(Zeghidour\net al.\n2021\n; Défossez\net al.\n2023\n; Dhariwal\net al.\n2020\n; Kreuk\net al.\n2023\n)\n; video generation is the instructive counter-case, and it separates two things that are easily conflated. Human-centric video models emit pixels and no audio at all\n(Chen\net al.\n2026a\n; Owens\net al.\n2016\n; Miao\net al.\n2026\n)\n, and the models that do emit sound alongside video produce a joint waveform\n(HaCohen\net al.\n2026\n; Team\net al.\n2026\n)\n, as does the video-to-audio line before them\n(Luo\net al.\n2023\n; Gan\net al.\n2020\n; Zhang\net al.\n2026\n)\n. That is\nsound\ngeneration, which is not music generation, and in either case a waveform offers no handle on key, chord, instrument or note: what a model can be asked to control is fixed by what it is asked to emit; a parallel line builds music- and audio-\nunderstanding\nmodels\n(Gardner\net al.\n2024\n; Liu\net al.\n2024b\n; Chu\net al.\n2023\n; Li\net al.\n2024\n; Ye\net al.\n2024\n)\n, one of which (Qwen3-Omni) supplies our captions. Appendix\nA\ntreats the closest of these in full.\nEvaluation of Symbolic Music Generation\nEvaluation here is fragmented: MidiCaps supplies the de-facto text–MIDI dataset\n(Melechovsky\net al.\n2024b\n)\nwithout a protocol; papers mix perplexity, feature histograms\n(Yang and Lerch\n2020\n)\nand rendered-audio scores\n(Kilgour\net al.\n2019\n)\n; FMD contributes a symbolic Fréchet distance over CLaMP-2\n(Retkowski\net al.\n2024\n)\nand CLaMP-3 a symbolic-native alignment score\n(Wu\net al.\n2025a\n)\n; surveys document the absence of standardized benchmarks\n(Xiong\net al.\n2023\n; Ma\net al.\n2024\n)\n; benchmarks for other structured outputs face the same problem\n(Guo\net al.\n2025\n; Fradet\net al.\n2023\n; Zhang\net al.\n2023a\n; Qian\net al.\n2026b\n; Chen\net al.\n2025b\n)\n. Two pitfalls matter for method papers: per-token loss is incomparable across vocabularies, and raw texture statistics ignore that encodings differ in what they can express at all. Our ceiling-anchored protocol addresses both.\nMethod\nOverview\nProblem definition.\nGiven a natural-language description\nc\nc\n, generate a symbolic score\ny\ny\n, notes with pitch, onset, duration, velocity and instrument, that is well-formed (decodable and renderable), matches the description, and matches the statistical profile of real music. A representation\nR\nR\nserializes\ny\ny\ninto a token sequence with decoder\nR\n−\n1\nR^{-1}\n; a text-to-music method is a choice of\nR\nR\nplus a procedure for training\np\nθ\n​\n(\nR\n​\n(\ny\n)\n|\nc\n)\np_{\\theta}(R(y)\\,|\\,c)\n. PMT’s pipeline (Figure\n1\n) has three interlocking components: the\nrepresentation\nencodes each piece into a flat music-token stream, the\nintegration recipe\nappends that stream to the caption and fine-tunes a pretrained backbone with the caption masked, and the\nevaluation protocol\nvalidates every design choice by training competing representations under the identical recipe. The recipe holds everything but the representation fixed, which is what lets the protocol attribute quality differences to the representation.\nFigure 2:\nOne excerpt through the encoding.\nTop:\nthe MIDI events as the file stores them.\nBottom:\nthe PMT token stream for the same notes.\nComponent 1: Performance-Timed Parametric Representation\nPMT serializes each note as an interpretable parameter tuple; Figure\n2\nwalks one excerpt through it, a four-note chord on one track followed by a single onset advance, from the file’s own\nnote_on\nevents to the token stream and back. The vocabulary comprises\nPITCH\n(128),\nDUR\n(200 bins at 10 ms, covering 10–2000 ms),\nVEL\n(32 levels),\nTSHIFT\n(100 bins at 10 ms; gaps beyond 1 s emit repeated shifts),\nTRACK\n(16), and\nPROG\n(129) plus four special tokens, 609 symbols total. Small named vocabularies of this kind are how other structured artifacts are made LLM-writable\n(Wu\net al.\n2023a\n; Xing\net al.\n2024\n)\n, and naming the parts is what makes an output editable after the fact\n(Poole\net al.\n2023\n; Xu\net al.\n2020\n)\n,\n≈\n\\approx\n4.0 tokens per note. Writing\n[\na\n,\nb\n)\n[a,b)\nfor a family’s integer index range and\n𝒮\n\\mathcal{S}\nfor the four special tokens, the music vocabulary is the disjoint union of the six parameter families,\n𝒱\nmusic\n=\n\\displaystyle\\mathcal{V}_{\\text{music}}=\nTRACK\n[\n0\n,\n16\n)\n⊔\nPROG\n[\n0\n,\n129\n)\n⊔\nPITCH\n[\n0\n,\n128\n)\n\\displaystyle\\ \\texttt{TRACK}_{[0,16)}\\sqcup\\texttt{PROG}_{[0,129)}\\sqcup\\texttt{PITCH}_{[0,128)}\n⊔\nDUR\n[\n1\n,\n201\n)\n⊔\nVEL\n[\n0\n,\n32\n)\n⊔\nTSHIFT", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 400, "totalLines": 12535}}}
