{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-846588c91c46ed1f", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1500\tm\n1501\t​\n1502\t(\n1503\tR\n1504\t−\n1505\t1\n1506\t​\n1507\t(\n1508\tR\n1509\t​\n1510\t(\n1511\tY\n1512\ttest\n1513\t)\n1514\t)\n1515\t)\n1516\t,\n1517\tA_{R}(m)=\\frac{m(\\hat{Y})}{\\mathrm{ceil}_{R}(m)},\\qquad\\mathrm{ceil}_{R}(m)=m\\!\\big(R^{-1}(R(Y_{\\text{test}}))\\big),\n1518\t(7)\n1519\tso the ceiling is what survives a\n1520\tmodel-free\n1521\tround-trip, the most of\n1522\tm\n1523\tm\n1524\tthat\n1525\tR\n1526\tR\n1527\tcan physically carry (a beat grid cannot round-trip sub-beat chords). Attainment separates expressiveness, a property of the\n1528\tformat\n1529\t, from learnability, the fraction a\n1530\tmodel\n1531\trealizes, the only fair basis for comparing formats of different capacity.\n1532\tGuarded metrics\n1533\t: FMD over CLaMP-2 for distributional fidelity, CLaMP-3 for caption alignment with all systems routed through one MIDI\n1534\t→\n1535\t\\to\n1536\tMTF path, and per-token loss used strictly within a representation, since across vocabularies it is meaningless.\n1537\tExperiments\n1538\tDataset.\n1539\tWe build and release a four-modality corpus of 86,598 real-music pieces, each aligned across an English caption, sliced MIDI, ABC and rendered audio, from four sources (The Session folk, GiantMIDI classical piano\n1540\t(Kong\n1541\tet al.\n1542\t2022\n1543\t)\n1544\t, a genre-balanced Lakh\n1545\t(Raffel\n1546\t2016\n1547\t)\n1548\tsubset, and jazz). Splits are frozen and source-stratified (84,576 train / 2,022 test, seed 42) and PMT-encoded lengths are heavy-tailed (median 835 tokens), motivating the 1024-token block that covers\n1549\t58\n1550\t%\n1551\t58\\%\n1552\tof pieces unclipped. Table\n1553\t33\n1554\tplaces the corpus among existing collections: the large raw-MIDI datasets supply scale but no language supervision, multi-track audio–MIDI sets pair synthesized audio but no captions, and the one captioned dataset (MidiCaps) is single-modality, whereas ours is the only corpus aligning\n1555\tfour\n1556\tmodalities per piece. Because the largest raw collections ship un-captioned, we apply the same pipeline to their deduplicated union and build a\n1557\tscaled\n1558\tcaptioned corpus of\n1559\t6.25M\n1560\tMIDI–caption pairs, the largest captioned symbolic dataset,\n1561\t37\n1562\t×\n1563\t37\\times\n1564\tMidiCaps, deduplicated against the frozen test split. The controlled experiments use the aligned 86.6k set for its per-case alignment; §\n1565\tN\n1566\tgives per-source counts, the caption pipeline and its quality-control filter, and redistribution terms.\n1567\tImplementation details.\n1568\tAll backbones are from the\n1569\tQwen3.5\n1570\tfamily (0.8B/2B/4B/27B and a 35B-A3B MoE), fine-tuned in a single SFT stage. Music vocabularies extend the tokenizer with mean-initialized embeddings; captions are truncated to 640 tokens and masked with\n1571\t−\n1572\t100\n1573\t-100\n1574\t. Training: 10k steps (\n1575\t≈\n1576\t\\approx\n1577\t1.9 epochs), effective batch 16, lr\n1578\t8\n1579\t×\n1580\t10\n1581\t−\n1582\t5\n1583\t8{\\times}10^{-5}\n1584\tcosine, 3% warmup, bf16, block 1024. Evaluation: temperature 0.95, top-k 60, up to 900 music tokens, music-range-constrained logits,\n1585\tn\n1586\t=\n1587\t100\n1588\tn{=}100\n1589\tfrozen-test captions per cell, up to 3 seeds (per-scale splits, vocabulary sizes and compute in Appendix\n1590\tL\n1591\t). Two cautions on reading FMD. It is comparable only\n1592\twithin\n1593\tone protocol: the same PMT-0.8B scores\n1594\t159\n1595\t159\n1596\tagainst the 500-piece stratified reference and\n1597\t114\n1598\t114\n1599\tagainst the 475-piece deduplicated one, and the PMT-vs-beat-grid\n1600\tratio\n1601\tranges\n1602\t1.6\n1603\t1.6\n1604\t–\n1605\t2.8\n1606\t×\n1607\t2.8\\times\n1608\tacross protocols, so the “\n1609\t≈\n1610\t2\n1611\t×\n1612\t{\\approx}2\\times\n1613\t” headline is a representative midpoint. And it carries sampling noise: a real-vs-real floor is\n1614\t43\n1615\t43\n1616\tat\n1617\tn\n1618\t=\n1619\t100\n1620\tn{=}100\n1621\t, while the PMT-vs-beat-grid gap (\n1622\t159\n1623\t159\n1624\tvs.\n1625\t272\n1626\t272\n1627\t) clears that floor. We run no per-comparison significance tests and coverage thins at scale, so the\n1628\tlarge\n1629\tgaps are the claim and finer orderings are trends.\n1630\tMain Results: PMT vs. Alternative Representations\n1631\tSize\n1632\tRepr.\n1633\tFMD\n1634\t↓\n1635\t\\downarrow\n1636\tJSD\n1637\t↓\n1638\ti\n1639\t​\n1640\to\n1641\t​\n1642\ti\n1643\t{}_{ioi}\\downarrow\n1644\tChord%\n1645\t→\n1646\t\\rightarrow\n1647\tAtt.\n1648\t↑\n1649\t\\uparrow\n1650\tReference: FMD 0, JSD\n1651\tioi\n1652\t0, chord-time 36.7%\n1653\t0.8B\n1654\tPMT\n1655\t159\n1656\t±\n1657\t\\pm\n1658\t8\n1659\t0.032\n1660\t±\n1661\t\\pm\n1662\t0.002\n1663\t36.2\n1664\t±\n1665\t\\pm\n1666\t5.6\n1667\t0.92\n1668\t0.8B\n1669\tBeat-TSD\n1670\t286\n1671\t±\n1672\t\\pm\n1673\t1\n1674\t0.152\n1675\t±\n1676\t\\pm\n1677\t0.008\n1678\t30.8\n1679\t±\n1680\t\\pm\n1681\t0.9\n1682\t0.87\n1683\t0.8B\n1684\tREMI\n1685\t285\n1686\t±\n1687\t\\pm\n1688\t1\n1689\t0.152\n1690\t±\n1691\t\\pm\n1692\t0.007\n1693\t30.2\n1694\t±\n1695\t\\pm\n1696\t1.7\n1697\t0.85\n1698\t0.8B\n1699\tMIDI-Like\n1700\t285\n1701\t±\n1702\t\\pm\n1703\t2\n1704\t0.160\n1705\t±\n1706\t\\pm\n1707\t0.006\n1708\t26.6\n1709\t±\n1710\t\\pm\n1711\t2.0\n1712\t0.75\n1713\t0.8B\n1714\tPerTok\n1715\t188\n1716\t±\n1717\t\\pm\n1718\t7\n1719\t0.011\n1720\t±\n1721\t\\pm\n1722\t0.001\n1723\t30.2\n1724\t±\n1725\t\\pm\n1726\t2.6\n1727\t0.86\n1728\t0.8B\n1729\tStructured\n1730\t287\n1731\t0.121\n1732\t30.3\n1733\t0.85\n1734\t0.8B\n1735\tABC\n1736\t406\n1737\t0.079\n1738\t16.2\n1739\t0.50\n1740\t27B\n1741\tPMT\n1742\t156\n1743\t0.048\n1744\t35.8\n1745\t±\n1746\t\\pm\n1747\t0.5\n1748\t0.92\n1749\t27B\n1750\tREMI\n1751\t272\n1752\t0.144\n1753\t±\n1754\t\\pm\n1755\t0.005\n1756\t28.8\n1757\t±\n1758\t\\pm\n1759\t0.4\n1760\t0.81\n1761\t27B\n1762\tMIDI-Like\n1763\t285\n1764\t0.148\n1765\t27.9\n1766\t0.78\n1767\tTable 1:\n1768\tControlled comparison on 100 frozen-test captions per cell (mean\n1769\t±\n1770\t\\pm\n1771\tstd over seeds; single value\n1772\t=\n1773\t=\n1774\tone seed); identical backbone, data, budget, decoding, validity 0.99–1.00.\n1775\tFMD\n1776\t↓\n1777\t\\downarrow\n1778\t=\n1779\t=\n1780\tCLaMP-2 Fréchet Music Distance;\n1781\tJSD\n1782\tioi\n1783\t↓\n1784\t\\downarrow\n1785\t=\n1786\t=\n1787\tinter-onset-interval divergence;\n1788\tChord%\n1789\t→\n1790\t\\rightarrow\n1791\t/\n1792\tAtt.\n1793\t↑\n1794\t\\uparrow\n1795\t=\n1796\t=\n1797\tchord-time closer-to-reference and its ceiling-normalized attainment, a coarser proxy that is mixture-confounded per source (Appendix\n1798\tG\n1799\t).\n1800\tTable\n1801\t1\n1802\tshows only the two extremes of the grid; the 2B, 4B and 9B blocks behave\n1803\tidentically and are in Appendix\n1804\tK\n1805\t. It orders the representations PMT\n1806\t>\n1807\t>\n1808\tBeat-TSD\n1809\t≈\n1810\t\\approx\n1811\tPerTok\n1812\t≈\n1813\t\\approx\n1814\tREMI\n1815\t>\n1816\t>\n1817\tMIDI-Like\n1818\t1\n1819\t1\n1820\t1\n1821\tThree similarly-named entities appear in this paper and are distinct:\n1822\tMIDI-Like\n1823\tis a MidiTok\n1824\ttokenization\n1825\t(a controlled-swap arm here);\n1826\tMIDI-LLM\n1827\t(Wu\n1828\tet al.\n1829\t2025b\n1830\t)\n1831\tand\n1832\tMIDILM\n1833\t(Li\n1834\tet al.\n1835\t2026\n1836\t)\n1837\tare two separate published\n1838\tsystems\n1839\tused as external baselines.\n1840\t≫\n1841\t\\gg\n1842\tABC. Performance resolution is not unique to PMT: PerTok, another performance-resolution encoding, joins PMT in the low-FMD/low-JSD\n1843\tioi\n1844\tcorner and even edges it on onset divergence (\n1845\t.011\n1846\t.011\n1847\tvs.\n1848\t.032\n1849\t.032\n1850\t), corroborating that performance resolution (\n1851\t10\n1852\t10\n1853\tms timing\n1854\tand\n1855\tper-note velocity), not the beat grid, is the binding distinction. PMT’s edge over PerTok is\n1856\tcompleteness\n1857\t, not timing: PerTok spends\n1858\t6.8\n1859\t6.8\n1860\ttokens/note against PMT’s\n1861\t4.0\n1862\t4.0\n1863\tand its stream is single-track, so the arrangement PMT carries in one stream is outside PerTok’s reach (Appendix\n1864\tK\n1865\t).\n1866\tChord-time is a coarse, mixture-confounded proxy.\n1867\tStress-testing our own headline statistic, a per-source breakdown (Appendix\n1868\tG\n1869\t) shows the aggregate flatters no arm uniformly: PMT\n1870\tover\n1871\t-harmonizes the folk majority (\n1872\t25\n1873\t%\n1874\t25\\%\n1875\tvs. a\n1876\t13\n1877\t%\n1878\t13\\%\n1879\treference) and\n1880\tunder\n1881\t-fills chordal classical (\n1882\t50\n1883\t%\n1884\t50\\%\n1885\tvs.\n1886\t82\n1887\t%\n1888\t82\\%\n1889\t), so its blended match (\n1890\t36\n1891\t%\n1892\t36\\%\n1893\tvs.\n1894\t36.7\n1895\t%\n1896\t36.7\\%\n1897\t) is partly cancellation across a\n1898\t69\n1899\t%\n1900\t69\\%\n1901\t-folk mixture. We therefore rest the quality claim on the full-distribution metrics, FMD and the timing/duration divergences, which are not chord-count cancellations. Unlike the chord-time cancellation, that advantage does\n1902\tnot\n1903\twash out per source: split into folk and non-folk, PMT leads Beat-TSD on FMD in\n1904\tboth\n1905\t(\n1906\t67\n1907\t67\n1908\tvs.\n1909\t220\n1910\t220\n1911\tand\n1912\t337\n1913\t337\n1914\tvs.\n1915\t492\n1916\t492\n1917\t).\n1918\tEfficiency and ceilings.\n1919\tPMT pays a moderate token cost (\n1920\t4.03\n1921\t4.03\n1922\ttokens/note against ABC’s\n1923\t2.84\n1924\t2.84\n1925\t) and converts it: it has the highest round-trip ceiling (\n1926\t39.1\n1927\t%\n1928\t39.1\\%\n1929\tchord-time vs.\n1930\t35.5\n1931\t%\n1932\t35.5\\%\n1933\tfor beat grids) and, at\n1934\t0.8\n1935\t0.8\n1936\tB, the highest attainment of it (\n1937\t.92\n1938\t.92\n1939\t), while compactness alone buys nothing, ABC being leanest and worst. The efficiency table, the model-free encode/decode probe and wall-clock generation rates are in Appendix\n1940\tJ\n1941\t.\n1942\tScaling Behavior\n1943\tThe clean reading of scale is cross-representation (Appendix\n1944\tK\n1945\t, Figure\n1946\t13\n1947\t): PMT’s FMD band (\n1948\t152\n1949\t152\n1950\t–\n1951\t159\n1952\t159\n1953\tacross\n1954\t0.8\n1955\t0.8\n1956\tB–\n1957\t27\n1958\t27\n1959\tB) lies entirely below every beat-grid arm’s (\n1960\t272\n1961\t272\n1962\t–\n1963\t286\n1964\t286\n1965\t), so the efficiency claim is a Pareto statement, not a fragile single point. The curves do not cross at\n1966\tany scale, so no amount of backbone size moves a beat grid into PMT’s distributional regime. Fitting FMD against\n1967\tlog\n1968\t10\n1969\t\\log_{10}\n1970\t-parameters, every arm’s slope is within noise of flat, leaving a near-constant\n1971\t≈\n1972\t125\n1973\t{\\approx}125\n1974\t-point PMT-vs-beat-grid offset that scale does not erode. We do not extrapolate a crossover from a fit this flat over\n1975\t1.5\n1976\t1.5\n1977\tdecades; the defensible statement is that there is\n1978\tno\n1979\tclosing trend within range, so representation, not parameter count, is the binding variable at every scale we measure.\n1980\tFrom-scratch validation.\n1981\tA web-pretrained backbone could favor some representations through prior exposure (it demonstrably favors ABC). Repeating the swap with a 26M decoder-only Transformer trained\n1982\tfrom scratch\n1983\ton MAESTRO\n1984\t(Hawthorne\n1985\tet al.\n1986\t2019\n1987\t)\n1988\t, changing only the tokenization, reproduces the ordering (PMT FMD\n1989\t125.1\n1990\t±\n1991\t11.7\n1992\t125.1\\pm 11.7\n1993\tover four seeds vs. REMI’s\n1994\t344.4\n1995\t±\n1996\t9.8\n1997\t344.4\\pm 9.8\n1998\t) and transfers to POP909\n1999\t(Wang\n2000\tet al.\n2001\t2020\n2002\t)\n2003\t, so two independent backbone families agree and the pretraining-prior confound is closed. The ordering also holds on\n2004\tmulti-instrument\n2005\tmaterial trained from scratch, the case a solo-piano reproduction leaves open: on a\n2006\t1\n2007\t,\n2008\t208\n2009\t1{,}208\n2010\t-piece genre-balanced subset PMT reaches FMD\n2011\t287.9\n2012\t±\n2013\t1.0\n2014\t287.9\\pm 1.0\n2015\t(three seeds) against\n2016\t395.3\n2017\t±\n2018\t1.1\n2019\t395.3\\pm 1.1\n2020\tfor Beat-TSD, with non-overlapping intervals (Appendix\n2021\tF\n2022\t).\n2023\tAblations and controls.\n2024\tEight controls (Appendix\n2025\tF\n2026\t) delimit the effect rather than only supporting it. Two matter most. Coarsening PMT’s timing to a beat-grid resolution on a fixed absolute grid moves its FMD toward the beat-grid arms, and removing PMT’s performance fields one at a time hurts under the from-scratch MAESTRO setup (dropping velocity degrades FMD from\n2027\t106.1\n2028\t106.1\n2029\tto\n2030\t697\n2031\t697\n2032\t), so the performance parameters are load-bearing. The recipe is not the driver: unmasked caption\n2033\t+\n2034\t+\n2035\tmusic loss and unconstrained decoding both leave the ordering intact, while the\n2036\trepresentation\n2037\tfamily does matter, swapping any MIDI-token encoding for ABC halves attainment (\n2038\t.69\n2039\t.69\n2040\t–\n2041\t.92\n2042\t→\n2043\t.46\n2044\t.92\\!\\to\\!.46\n2045\t–\n2046\t.51\n2047\t.51\n2048\t) and cuts polyphony (\n2049\t4.1\n2050\t4.1\n2051\t–\n2052\t5.8\n2053\t→\n2054\t3.0\n2055\t5.8\\!\\to\\!3.0\n2056\t) under identical conditions. Training-seed std is\n2057\t≤\n2058\t\\leq\n2059\t2pp for baselines and\n2060\t≈\n2061\t6\n2062\t{\\approx}6\n2063\tpp for PMT at\n2064\t0.8\n2065\t0.8\n2066\tB; repeated evaluation bounds sampling noise at\n2067\t≈\n2068\t2.5\n2069\t{\\approx}2.5\n2070\tpp.\n2071\tComparison with Published Systems\n2072\tRun through our harness on identical captions,\n2073\tboth\n2074\tpublished systems imprint their training distributions rather than following the caption: MIDI-LLM emits\n2075\t72\n2076\t%\n2077\t72\\%\n2078\tchord-time and text2midi\n2079\t66\n2080\t%\n2081\t66\\%\n2082\twith\n2083\t17.3\n2084\t17.3\n2085\tmax-polyphony, near-invariant to what the caption asks for (Appendix\n2086\tI\n2087\t, which also shows the full generation pipeline side by side).\n2088\tPublic-benchmark transfer (MidiCaps).\n2089\tWe evaluate on 200 captions from MidiCaps, text2midi’s training domain, disjoint from our training subset, with identical extractors and the text2midi adherence metrics (Tempo-Bin, TBT\n2090\t±\n2091\t\\pm\n2092\t10%; Correct-Key).\n2093\tSystem\n2094\tFMD\n2095\t↓\n2096\t\\downarrow\n2097\tValid.\n2098\tTBT\n2099\tCK\n2100\tIF1\n2101\tPoly\n2102\tPMT-4B\n2103\t86.6k\n2104\t196\n2105\t1.00\n2106\t.44\n2107\t.10\n2108\t.46\n2109\t10.6\n2110\tPMT-4B\n2111\t6.25M\n2112\t211\n2113\t1.00\n2114\t.44\n2115\t.17\n2116\t.47\n2117\t10.2\n2118\tMIDI-LLM\n2119\t314\n2120\t1.00\n2121\t.44\n2122\t.32\n2123\t.63\n2124\t11.7\n2125\tMIDILM\n2126\t351\n2127\t1.00\n2128\t.70\n2129\t.39\n2130\t.54\n2131\t16.8\n2132\tAmadeus\n2133\t380\n2134\t1.00\n2135\t.90\n2136\t.57\n2137\t.62\n2138\t72.1\n2139\ttext2midi\n2140\t421\n2141\t0.99\n2142\t.44\n2143\t–\n2144\t†\n2145\t.34\n2146\t18.5\n2147\tChatMusician\n2148\t460\n2149\t1.00\n2150\t.45\n2151\t.36\n2152\t.29\n2153\t0\n2154\t3.8\n2155\tReal reference\n2156\t0\n2157\t–\n2158\t–\n2159\t–\n2160\t–\n2161\t6.1\n2162\tTable 2:\n2163\tMidiCaps public test, 200 captions disjoint from our training data, identical extractors and one FMD protocol for every row.\n2164\tFMD\n2165\t↓\n2166\t\\downarrow\n2167\t=\n2168\t=\n2169\tdistance to the real note distribution;\n2170\tTBT\n2171\t=\n2172\t=\n2173\ttempo within\n2174\t±\n2175\t\\pm\n2176\t10% of the caption\n2177\t↑\n2178\t\\uparrow\n2179\t;\n2180\tCK\n2181\t=\n2182\t=\n2183\tcorrect-key rate\n2184\t↑\n2185\t\\uparrow\n2186\t;\n2187\tIF1\n2188\t=\n2189\t=\n2190\tinstrument-F1 against the caption’s named instruments\n2191\t↑\n2192\t\\uparrow\n2193\t;\n2194\tPoly\n2195\t=\n2196\t=\n2197\tmax-polyphony. Both PMT rows are zero-shot. Full table, the PMT-27B and zero-shot-LLM rows and the CLAP/audio comparison: Appendix\n2198\tI\n2199\t, Table\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fa787f0e-de86-4690-a3fa-8cf13e967722", "uuid": "48db6b1d-dee3-4e11-9da3-753c3a3d149c", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T10:02:19.331Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/agogic_text.txt", "content": "m\n​\n(\nR\n−\n1\n​\n(\nR\n​\n(\nY\ntest\n)\n)\n)\n,\nA_{R}(m)=\\frac{m(\\hat{Y})}{\\mathrm{ceil}_{R}(m)},\\qquad\\mathrm{ceil}_{R}(m)=m\\!\\big(R^{-1}(R(Y_{\\text{test}}))\\big),\n(7)\nso the ceiling is what survives a\nmodel-free\nround-trip, the most of\nm\nm\nthat\nR\nR\ncan physically carry (a beat grid cannot round-trip sub-beat chords). Attainment separates expressiveness, a property of the\nformat\n, from learnability, the fraction a\nmodel\nrealizes, the only fair basis for comparing formats of different capacity.\nGuarded metrics\n: FMD over CLaMP-2 for distributional fidelity, CLaMP-3 for caption alignment with all systems routed through one MIDI\n→\n\\to\nMTF path, and per-token loss used strictly within a representation, since across vocabularies it is meaningless.\nExperiments\nDataset.\nWe build and release a four-modality corpus of 86,598 real-music pieces, each aligned across an English caption, sliced MIDI, ABC and rendered audio, from four sources (The Session folk, GiantMIDI classical piano\n(Kong\net al.\n2022\n)\n, a genre-balanced Lakh\n(Raffel\n2016\n)\nsubset, and jazz). Splits are frozen and source-stratified (84,576 train / 2,022 test, seed 42) and PMT-encoded lengths are heavy-tailed (median 835 tokens), motivating the 1024-token block that covers\n58\n%\n58\\%\nof pieces unclipped. Table\n33\nplaces the corpus among existing collections: the large raw-MIDI datasets supply scale but no language supervision, multi-track audio–MIDI sets pair synthesized audio but no captions, and the one captioned dataset (MidiCaps) is single-modality, whereas ours is the only corpus aligning\nfour\nmodalities per piece. Because the largest raw collections ship un-captioned, we apply the same pipeline to their deduplicated union and build a\nscaled\ncaptioned corpus of\n6.25M\nMIDI–caption pairs, the largest captioned symbolic dataset,\n37\n×\n37\\times\nMidiCaps, deduplicated against the frozen test split. The controlled experiments use the aligned 86.6k set for its per-case alignment; §\nN\ngives per-source counts, the caption pipeline and its quality-control filter, and redistribution terms.\nImplementation details.\nAll backbones are from the\nQwen3.5\nfamily (0.8B/2B/4B/27B and a 35B-A3B MoE), fine-tuned in a single SFT stage. Music vocabularies extend the tokenizer with mean-initialized embeddings; captions are truncated to 640 tokens and masked with\n−\n100\n-100\n. Training: 10k steps (\n≈\n\\approx\n1.9 epochs), effective batch 16, lr\n8\n×\n10\n−\n5\n8{\\times}10^{-5}\ncosine, 3% warmup, bf16, block 1024. Evaluation: temperature 0.95, top-k 60, up to 900 music tokens, music-range-constrained logits,\nn\n=\n100\nn{=}100\nfrozen-test captions per cell, up to 3 seeds (per-scale splits, vocabulary sizes and compute in Appendix\nL\n). Two cautions on reading FMD. It is comparable only\nwithin\none protocol: the same PMT-0.8B scores\n159\n159\nagainst the 500-piece stratified reference and\n114\n114\nagainst the 475-piece deduplicated one, and the PMT-vs-beat-grid\nratio\nranges\n1.6\n1.6\n–\n2.8\n×\n2.8\\times\nacross protocols, so the “\n≈\n2\n×\n{\\approx}2\\times\n” headline is a representative midpoint. And it carries sampling noise: a real-vs-real floor is\n43\n43\nat\nn\n=\n100\nn{=}100\n, while the PMT-vs-beat-grid gap (\n159\n159\nvs.\n272\n272\n) clears that floor. We run no per-comparison significance tests and coverage thins at scale, so the\nlarge\ngaps are the claim and finer orderings are trends.\nMain Results: PMT vs. Alternative Representations\nSize\nRepr.\nFMD\n↓\n\\downarrow\nJSD\n↓\ni\n​\no\n​\ni\n{}_{ioi}\\downarrow\nChord%\n→\n\\rightarrow\nAtt.\n↑\n\\uparrow\nReference: FMD 0, JSD\nioi\n0, chord-time 36.7%\n0.8B\nPMT\n159\n±\n\\pm\n8\n0.032\n±\n\\pm\n0.002\n36.2\n±\n\\pm\n5.6\n0.92\n0.8B\nBeat-TSD\n286\n±\n\\pm\n1\n0.152\n±\n\\pm\n0.008\n30.8\n±\n\\pm\n0.9\n0.87\n0.8B\nREMI\n285\n±\n\\pm\n1\n0.152\n±\n\\pm\n0.007\n30.2\n±\n\\pm\n1.7\n0.85\n0.8B\nMIDI-Like\n285\n±\n\\pm\n2\n0.160\n±\n\\pm\n0.006\n26.6\n±\n\\pm\n2.0\n0.75\n0.8B\nPerTok\n188\n±\n\\pm\n7\n0.011\n±\n\\pm\n0.001\n30.2\n±\n\\pm\n2.6\n0.86\n0.8B\nStructured\n287\n0.121\n30.3\n0.85\n0.8B\nABC\n406\n0.079\n16.2\n0.50\n27B\nPMT\n156\n0.048\n35.8\n±\n\\pm\n0.5\n0.92\n27B\nREMI\n272\n0.144\n±\n\\pm\n0.005\n28.8\n±\n\\pm\n0.4\n0.81\n27B\nMIDI-Like\n285\n0.148\n27.9\n0.78\nTable 1:\nControlled comparison on 100 frozen-test captions per cell (mean\n±\n\\pm\nstd over seeds; single value\n=\n=\none seed); identical backbone, data, budget, decoding, validity 0.99–1.00.\nFMD\n↓\n\\downarrow\n=\n=\nCLaMP-2 Fréchet Music Distance;\nJSD\nioi\n↓\n\\downarrow\n=\n=\ninter-onset-interval divergence;\nChord%\n→\n\\rightarrow\n/\nAtt.\n↑\n\\uparrow\n=\n=\nchord-time closer-to-reference and its ceiling-normalized attainment, a coarser proxy that is mixture-confounded per source (Appendix\nG\n).\nTable\n1\nshows only the two extremes of the grid; the 2B, 4B and 9B blocks behave\nidentically and are in Appendix\nK\n. It orders the representations PMT\n>\n>\nBeat-TSD\n≈\n\\approx\nPerTok\n≈\n\\approx\nREMI\n>\n>\nMIDI-Like\n1\n1\n1\nThree similarly-named entities appear in this paper and are distinct:\nMIDI-Like\nis a MidiTok\ntokenization\n(a controlled-swap arm here);\nMIDI-LLM\n(Wu\net al.\n2025b\n)\nand\nMIDILM\n(Li\net al.\n2026\n)\nare two separate published\nsystems\nused as external baselines.\n≫\n\\gg\nABC. Performance resolution is not unique to PMT: PerTok, another performance-resolution encoding, joins PMT in the low-FMD/low-JSD\nioi\ncorner and even edges it on onset divergence (\n.011\n.011\nvs.\n.032\n.032\n), corroborating that performance resolution (\n10\n10\nms timing\nand\nper-note velocity), not the beat grid, is the binding distinction. PMT’s edge over PerTok is\ncompleteness\n, not timing: PerTok spends\n6.8\n6.8\ntokens/note against PMT’s\n4.0\n4.0\nand its stream is single-track, so the arrangement PMT carries in one stream is outside PerTok’s reach (Appendix\nK\n).\nChord-time is a coarse, mixture-confounded proxy.\nStress-testing our own headline statistic, a per-source breakdown (Appendix\nG\n) shows the aggregate flatters no arm uniformly: PMT\nover\n-harmonizes the folk majority (\n25\n%\n25\\%\nvs. a\n13\n%\n13\\%\nreference) and\nunder\n-fills chordal classical (\n50\n%\n50\\%\nvs.\n82\n%\n82\\%\n), so its blended match (\n36\n%\n36\\%\nvs.\n36.7\n%\n36.7\\%\n) is partly cancellation across a\n69\n%\n69\\%\n-folk mixture. We therefore rest the quality claim on the full-distribution metrics, FMD and the timing/duration divergences, which are not chord-count cancellations. Unlike the chord-time cancellation, that advantage does\nnot\nwash out per source: split into folk and non-folk, PMT leads Beat-TSD on FMD in\nboth\n(\n67\n67\nvs.\n220\n220\nand\n337\n337\nvs.\n492\n492\n).\nEfficiency and ceilings.\nPMT pays a moderate token cost (\n4.03\n4.03\ntokens/note against ABC’s\n2.84\n2.84\n) and converts it: it has the highest round-trip ceiling (\n39.1\n%\n39.1\\%\nchord-time vs.\n35.5\n%\n35.5\\%\nfor beat grids) and, at\n0.8\n0.8\nB, the highest attainment of it (\n.92\n.92\n), while compactness alone buys nothing, ABC being leanest and worst. The efficiency table, the model-free encode/decode probe and wall-clock generation rates are in Appendix\nJ\n.\nScaling Behavior\nThe clean reading of scale is cross-representation (Appendix\nK\n, Figure\n13\n): PMT’s FMD band (\n152\n152\n–\n159\n159\nacross\n0.8\n0.8\nB–\n27\n27\nB) lies entirely below every beat-grid arm’s (\n272\n272\n–\n286\n286\n), so the efficiency claim is a Pareto statement, not a fragile single point. The curves do not cross at\nany scale, so no amount of backbone size moves a beat grid into PMT’s distributional regime. Fitting FMD against\nlog\n10\n\\log_{10}\n-parameters, every arm’s slope is within noise of flat, leaving a near-constant\n≈\n125\n{\\approx}125\n-point PMT-vs-beat-grid offset that scale does not erode. We do not extrapolate a crossover from a fit this flat over\n1.5\n1.5\ndecades; the defensible statement is that there is\nno\nclosing trend within range, so representation, not parameter count, is the binding variable at every scale we measure.\nFrom-scratch validation.\nA web-pretrained backbone could favor some representations through prior exposure (it demonstrably favors ABC). Repeating the swap with a 26M decoder-only Transformer trained\nfrom scratch\non MAESTRO\n(Hawthorne\net al.\n2019\n)\n, changing only the tokenization, reproduces the ordering (PMT FMD\n125.1\n±\n11.7\n125.1\\pm 11.7\nover four seeds vs. REMI’s\n344.4\n±\n9.8\n344.4\\pm 9.8\n) and transfers to POP909\n(Wang\net al.\n2020\n)\n, so two independent backbone families agree and the pretraining-prior confound is closed. The ordering also holds on\nmulti-instrument\nmaterial trained from scratch, the case a solo-piano reproduction leaves open: on a\n1\n,\n208\n1{,}208\n-piece genre-balanced subset PMT reaches FMD\n287.9\n±\n1.0\n287.9\\pm 1.0\n(three seeds) against\n395.3\n±\n1.1\n395.3\\pm 1.1\nfor Beat-TSD, with non-overlapping intervals (Appendix\nF\n).\nAblations and controls.\nEight controls (Appendix\nF\n) delimit the effect rather than only supporting it. Two matter most. Coarsening PMT’s timing to a beat-grid resolution on a fixed absolute grid moves its FMD toward the beat-grid arms, and removing PMT’s performance fields one at a time hurts under the from-scratch MAESTRO setup (dropping velocity degrades FMD from\n106.1\n106.1\nto\n697\n697\n), so the performance parameters are load-bearing. The recipe is not the driver: unmasked caption\n+\n+\nmusic loss and unconstrained decoding both leave the ordering intact, while the\nrepresentation\nfamily does matter, swapping any MIDI-token encoding for ABC halves attainment (\n.69\n.69\n–\n.92\n→\n.46\n.92\\!\\to\\!.46\n–\n.51\n.51\n) and cuts polyphony (\n4.1\n4.1\n–\n5.8\n→\n3.0\n5.8\\!\\to\\!3.0\n) under identical conditions. Training-seed std is\n≤\n\\leq\n2pp for baselines and\n≈\n6\n{\\approx}6\npp for PMT at\n0.8\n0.8\nB; repeated evaluation bounds sampling noise at\n≈\n2.5\n{\\approx}2.5\npp.\nComparison with Published Systems\nRun through our harness on identical captions,\nboth\npublished systems imprint their training distributions rather than following the caption: MIDI-LLM emits\n72\n%\n72\\%\nchord-time and text2midi\n66\n%\n66\\%\nwith\n17.3\n17.3\nmax-polyphony, near-invariant to what the caption asks for (Appendix\nI\n, which also shows the full generation pipeline side by side).\nPublic-benchmark transfer (MidiCaps).\nWe evaluate on 200 captions from MidiCaps, text2midi’s training domain, disjoint from our training subset, with identical extractors and the text2midi adherence metrics (Tempo-Bin, TBT\n±\n\\pm\n10%; Correct-Key).\nSystem\nFMD\n↓\n\\downarrow\nValid.\nTBT\nCK\nIF1\nPoly\nPMT-4B\n86.6k\n196\n1.00\n.44\n.10\n.46\n10.6\nPMT-4B\n6.25M\n211\n1.00\n.44\n.17\n.47\n10.2\nMIDI-LLM\n314\n1.00\n.44\n.32\n.63\n11.7\nMIDILM\n351\n1.00\n.70\n.39\n.54\n16.8\nAmadeus\n380\n1.00\n.90\n.57\n.62\n72.1\ntext2midi\n421\n0.99\n.44\n–\n†\n.34\n18.5\nChatMusician\n460\n1.00\n.45\n.36\n.29\n0\n3.8\nReal reference\n0\n–\n–\n–\n–\n6.1\nTable 2:\nMidiCaps public test, 200 captions disjoint from our training data, identical extractors and one FMD protocol for every row.\nFMD\n↓\n\\downarrow\n=\n=\ndistance to the real note distribution;\nTBT\n=\n=\ntempo within\n±\n\\pm\n10% of the caption\n↑\n\\uparrow\n;\nCK\n=\n=\ncorrect-key rate\n↑\n\\uparrow\n;\nIF1\n=\n=\ninstrument-F1 against the caption’s named instruments\n↑\n\\uparrow\n;\nPoly\n=\n=\nmax-polyphony. Both PMT rows are zero-shot. Full table, the PMT-27B and zero-shot-LLM rows and the CLAP/audio comparison: Appendix\nI\n, Table", "numLines": 700, "startLine": 1500, "totalLines": 12535}}}
