{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b7157906084b91ce", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2200\t24\n2201\t.\n2202\t†\n2203\ttext2midi’s decoded MIDI is unparseable by the music21 key-finder.\n2204\tTwo readings. First,\n2205\tevery\n2206\tmodel, ours included, regresses toward its training mean (PMT over-harmonizes folk, Appendix\n2207\tG\n2208\t); the pathology we diagnose is caption-\n2209\tinvariance\n2210\t, and it is domain-invariant, MIDI-LLM emits a near-identical distribution (\n2211\t71\n2212\t%\n2213\t71\\%\n2214\tvs.\n2215\t72\n2216\t%\n2217\t72\\%\n2218\tchord-time) on two disjoint caption domains and does not improve with scale, whereas PMT shifts with the testbed and transfers zero-shot to parity on theirs (Figure\n2219\t10\n2220\t). A “more-texture-is-better” reading would reward MIDI-LLM’s constant\n2221\t71\n2222\t71\n2223\t–\n2224\t72\n2225\t%\n2226\t72\\%\n2227\t; scoring distance to each domain’s own real reference is what exposes it as caption-invariance instead, and MIDI-LLM sits near the MidiCaps reference only because MidiCaps\n2228\tis\n2229\tits home distribution, which is why the test is run in both directions. Second, on the shared tempo axis in-domain text2midi (\n2230\t.44\n2231\t.44\n2232\t) buys\n2233\tno\n2234\tadvantage over zero-shot PMT (\n2235\t.44\n2236\t.44\n2237\t); what separates systems is the explicit-attribute axes, where systems that condition on them directly lead. Single-domain benchmarks favor systems trained on that domain; bidirectional evaluation is the corrective.\n2238\tWhere PMT leads and where it does not.\n2239\tTable\n2240\t2\n2241\tseparates two axes that are usually reported as one. On distance to the real note distribution, the axis our thesis targets, PMT is decisively closest: same-protocol FMD\n2242\t196\n2243\t196\n2244\tfor the 86.6k-trained PMT-4B and\n2245\t211\n2246\t211\n2247\tfor the 6.25M one-epoch model, against\n2248\t314\n2249\t314\n2250\t–\n2251\t460\n2252\t460\n2253\tfor every published system,\n2254\t1.5\n2255\t1.5\n2256\t–\n2257\t2.3\n2258\t×\n2259\t2.3\\times\n2260\tfarther. On caption-attribute adherence PMT trails: MIDILM\n2261\t(Li\n2262\tet al.\n2263\t2026\n2264\t)\n2265\tand Amadeus\n2266\t(Su\n2267\tet al.\n2268\t2026\n2269\t)\n2270\tlead tempo and key (Amadeus TBT\n2271\t.90\n2272\t.90\n2273\t/CK\n2274\t.57\n2275\t.57\n2276\tvs. PMT\n2277\t.44\n2278\t.44\n2279\t/\n2280\t.17\n2281\t.17\n2282\t), and zero-shot general LLMs lead key adherence by copying the stated key (GPT-5.5 CK\n2283\t.66\n2284\t.66\n2285\t) while being playable for only\n2286\t58\n2287\t%\n2288\t58\\%\n2289\tof captions. No competitor is both adherent\n2290\tand\n2291\tclose-to-real: the adherence leaders reach it by over-texturing, at\n2292\t16.8\n2293\t16.8\n2294\tand\n2295\t72.1\n2296\t72.1\n2297\tmax-polyphony against a\n2298\t6.1\n2299\t6.1\n2300\treference. Cross-modal comparison against audio generators does not cleanly rank quality on this task, caption-matched real music scores\n2301\tlowest\n2302\ton CLAP (\n2303\t31.5\n2304\t31.5\n2305\t), so we report but do not rank by it (Appendix\n2306\tI\n2307\t).\n2308\tRobustness to piece complexity.\n2309\tPublic text-to-MIDI benchmarks skew short, so we also build a\n2310\ttiered\n2311\tbenchmark of 240 real pieces stratified by PMT-token length into four levels, each with its own real reference. PMT-4B and MIDILM both stay\n2312\t100\n2313\t%\n2314\t100\\%\n2315\tvalid at every tier, but PMT is closer to the per-tier real distribution at\n2316\tevery\n2317\tlevel (\n2318\t298\n2319\t/\n2320\t348\n2321\t/\n2322\t357\n2323\t/\n2324\t396\n2325\t298/348/357/396\n2326\tvs.\n2327\t422\n2328\t/\n2329\t378\n2330\t/\n2331\t377\n2332\t/\n2333\t436\n2334\t422/378/377/436\n2335\t) while MIDILM over-textures throughout, so the distributional advantage persists across complexity and degrades gracefully with length (Appendix\n2336\tJ\n2337\t).\n2338\tQualitative Results and Perceptual Scope\n2339\tPMT generations are caption-conditioned in texture: folk captions yield single-line dance tunes, while classical, jazz and pop captions yield sustained chordal writing with up to\n2340\t13\n2341\t13\n2342\t–\n2343\t17\n2344\t17\n2345\tsimultaneous notes. Appendix\n2346\tC\n2347\tshows the same caption realized by each representation, in piano roll and engraved: the beat-grid arms collapse to sparser tracks and ABC to a two-staff keyboard reduction under identical backbone and budget, so the representation, not the backbone, bounds the notated texture. An automatic listener (Qwen3-Omni-30B) is a\n2348\tweak\n2349\tproxy whose signal is material-dependent: it prefers micro-timing on expressive solo piano (\n2350\t60\n2351\t%\n2352\t60\\%\n2353\t,\n2354\t54\n2355\t/\n2356\t90\n2357\t54/90\n2358\t,\n2359\tp\n2360\t=\n2361\t0.07\n2362\tp{=}0.07\n2363\t) but not on the folk-heavy generated contrast (\n2364\t42\n2365\t%\n2366\t42\\%\n2367\t), so we make\n2368\tno\n2369\tperceptual claim from it. A pre-registered human study on expressive material is collecting; we report no perceptual result from it until it reaches its target sample, so the timing claim here stays distributional (Appendix\n2370\tH\n2371\t).\n2372\tWe probe audibility with an automatic listener (Qwen3-Omni-30B) and read it as a\n2373\tweak\n2374\tproxy that scopes where a human study should look rather than delivering a verdict. The signal is\n2375\tmaterial-dependent\n2376\t: on a model-free control isolating micro-timing on expressive solo piano the judge prefers the micro-timing-preserving version\n2377\t60\n2378\t%\n2379\t60\\%\n2380\tof the time (\n2381\t54\n2382\t/\n2383\t90\n2384\t54/90\n2385\t, directional,\n2386\tp\n2387\t=\n2388\t0.07\n2389\tp{=}0.07\n2390\t), while on the folk-heavy generated contrast it does\n2391\tnot\n2392\tprefer PMT (\n2393\t42\n2394\t%\n2395\t42\\%\n2396\t), consistent with a source that is already grid-quantized. We therefore make\n2397\tno\n2398\tperceptual claim from this probe. A human listening study on the material the probe points at is underway; the instrument, the pre-registered analysis and the response template are in Appendix\n2399\tH\n2400\t, and full listener details in Appendix\n2401\tH\n2402\t.\n2403\tLimitations\n2404\tFive limitations bound our claims; supporting numbers are in Appendix\n2405\tM\n2406\t.\n2407\t(1) Near-duplicate splits\n2408\t: the folk source has multiple community “settings” of one tune and our split is setting-level, so near-duplicates can straddle train/test and inflate held-out likelihood; we exclude\n2409\t>\n2410\t>\n2411\t4B points from NLL analyses, and generation-side metrics survive a title-level dedup control.\n2412\t(2) Audibility untested\n2413\t: the human study is still collecting, and our automatic proxy on the representation contrast is inconclusive, so we assert no perceptual difference in either direction.\n2414\t(3) Seed coverage at scale\n2415\t: 27B rows are two-seed, 35B-A3B single-seed.\n2416\t(4) Caption adherence\n2417\t: PMT’s key adherence is weak (CK\n2418\t.10\n2419\t.10\n2420\tvs. MIDI-LLM’s\n2421\t.32\n2422\t.32\n2423\t), but this is an absolute-key\n2424\tconditioning\n2425\tgap rather than tonal incompetence, and a decode-time attribute constraint more than doubles\n2426\tboth\n2427\tinstrument-F1 (\n2428\t.28\n2429\t→\n2430\t.60\n2431\t.28\\!\\to\\!.60\n2432\t) and Correct-Key (\n2433\t.16\n2434\t→\n2435\t.35\n2436\t.16\\!\\to\\!.35\n2437\t) at unchanged validity and no distributional cost, without retraining; steering a generator with language rather than retraining it is a recurring pattern\n2438\t(Lu\n2439\tet al.\n2440\t2023\n2441\t; Wu and Sun\n2442\t2023\n2443\t)\n2444\t. Our captions are also machine-generated by a single captioner and audited for prompt leakage but not factual accuracy; describing audiovisual content in language is an active task in its own right\n2445\t(Gardner\n2446\tet al.\n2447\t2024\n2448\t; Li\n2449\tet al.\n2450\t2024\n2451\t; Chu\n2452\tet al.\n2453\t2023\n2454\t)\n2455\t, and the accuracy of that description bounds any corpus built this way.\n2456\t(5) Corpus scale in the controlled study\n2457\t: the experiments use the 86.6k four-modality set, trading raw count for per-case alignment; as a first step to scale, a PMT-4B trained one epoch on the 6.25M corpus cuts held-out FMD\n2458\t2.0\n2459\t×\n2460\t2.0\\times\n2461\t(\n2462\t291.7\n2463\t→\n2464\t144.8\n2465\t291.7\\!\\to\\!144.8\n2466\t) and lifts MidiCaps key adherence (CK\n2467\t.10\n2468\t→\n2469\t.17\n2470\t.10\\!\\to\\!.17\n2471\t) at a\n2472\t15\n2473\t15\n2474\t-point FMD cost there and nearly doubles key adherence. We report that one-epoch model, not a converged one.\n2475\tConclusion\n2476\tWe asked whether, for an LLM extended to symbolic music, the token representation or the model’s size sets the ceiling on distributional fidelity, and answered with a controlled swap: fix the backbone, data, budget and decoding, and change only the representation. The representation wins. A performance-resolution stream we release (PMT) roughly halves the Fréchet Music Distance of every beat grid, is not overtaken by a\n2477\t34\n2478\t×\n2479\t34\\times\n2480\tparameter increase, and reproduces on a 26M from-scratch backbone and on a second performance-resolution tokenizer, so the effect belongs to the representation\n2481\tclass\n2482\t, not one vocabulary. We are careful about scope: the gap is distributional, robust to onset coarse-quantization but shown neither audible by our automatic probe nor better-conditioned, and PMT trails dedicated systems on caption adherence, a decode-time conditioning problem we already partly close at no distributional cost. The lesson outlasts music: how a new modality is serialized can dominate parameter count, and a controlled swap establishes this before any model is scaled, wherever a generative model is asked for a structured artifact rather than a dense signal\n2483\t(Xu\n2484\tet al.\n2485\t2020\n2486\t; Wu\n2487\tet al.\n2488\t2023a\n2489\t)\n2490\t. We release the datasets, harness and 25+ checkpoints so the next representation claim can be\n2491\tmeasured\n2492\t, not asserted.\n2493\tReferences\n2494\tA. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos,\n2495\tet al.\n2496\t(2023)\n2497\tMusicLM: generating music from text\n2498\t.\n2499\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325\n2500\t.\n2501\tCited by:\n2502\tAppendix N\n2503\t,\n2504\tTable 34\n2505\t.\n2506\tJ. Bai\n2507\tet al.\n2508\t(2024)\n2509\tAudioSetCaps: an enriched audio-caption dataset using automated generation pipeline\n2510\t.\n2511\tIn\n2512\tNeurIPS Workshop\n2513\t,\n2514\tCited by:\n2515\tAppendix N\n2516\t,\n2517\tTable 34\n2518\t.\n2519\tK. Bhandari, S. Chang, A. Roy, F. Ronchini, E. Benetos, D. Herremans, and S. Colton (2026)\n2520\tText2Score: generating sheet music from textual prompts\n2521\t.\n2522\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2605.13431\n2523\t.\n2524\tCited by:\n2525\t§A.2\n2526\t,\n2527\tText-Conditioned Symbolic Music Generation\n2528\t.\n2529\tK. Bhandari, A. Roy, K. Wang, G. Puri, S. Colton, and D. Herremans (2025)\n2530\tText2midi: generating symbolic music from captions\n2531\t.\n2532\tIn\n2533\tProceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence\n2534\t,\n2535\tVol.\n2536\t39\n2537\t,\n2538\tpp. 23478–23486\n2539\t.\n2540\tCited by:\n2541\t§A.2\n2542\t,\n2543\tIntroduction\n2544\t,\n2545\tText-Conditioned Symbolic Music Generation\n2546\t.\n2547\tL. Bradshaw and S. Colton (2025)\n2548\tAria-MIDI: a dataset of piano MIDI files for symbolic music modeling\n2549\t.\n2550\tIn\n2551\tInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n2552\t,\n2553\tVol.\n2554\t2025\n2555\t,\n2556\tpp. 99894–99910\n2557\t.\n2558\tCited by:\n2559\tAppendix N\n2560\t,\n2561\tTable 33\n2562\t,\n2563\tTable 33\n2564\t,\n2565\tTable 36\n2566\t.\n2567\tJ. Chen, M. Chen, J. Xu, X. Li, J. Dong, M. Sun, P. Jiang, H. Li, Y. Yang, H. Zhao, X. Long, and R. Huang (2026a)\n2568\tDanceTogether: generating interactive multi-person video without identity drifting\n2569\t.\n2570\tIn\n2571\tThe Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations\n2572\t,\n2573\tExternal Links:\n2574\tLink\n2575\tCited by:\n2576\tText-Conditioned Symbolic Music Generation\n2577\t.\n2578\tJ. Chen, X. Chen, H. Zhang, Z. Qiao, S. Zhang, Y. Li, R. Huang, S. Li, Y. Sheng, J. Zhu, and H. Zhao (2026b)\n2579\tEngine-native editable 3d world reconstruction with objects and lighting\n2580\t.\n2581\tExternal Links:\n2582\t2607.20889\n2583\t,\n2584\tLink\n2585\tCited by:\n2586\tSymbolic Music Representations\n2587\t.\n2588\tJ. Chen, K. Gao, Y. Cui, M. Sun, M. Chen, S. Wang, X. Long, F. Ma, Q. Tian, H. Zhao, and R. Huang (2026c)\n2589\tLottieGPT: tokenizing vector animation for autoregressive generation\n2590\t.\n2591\tIn\n2592\tProceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)\n2593\t,\n2594\tpp. 31639–31651\n2595\t.\n2596\tCited by:\n2597\tIntroduction\n2598\t.\n2599\tJ. Chen, X. Li, M. Chen, B. Zhang, H. Zhang, Y. Xu, Y. Cui, F. Weng, F. Ma, Q. Tian, R. Huang, and H. Zhao (2026d)\n2600\tPairCoder++: pair programming as a universal paradigm for verified code-driven multimodal and structured-artifact generation\n2601\t.\n2602\tExternal Links:\n2603\t2607.01883\n2604\t,\n2605\tLink\n2606\tCited by:\n2607\tText-Conditioned Symbolic Music Generation\n2608\t.\n2609\tJ. Chen, X. Li, Y. Xu, Y. Cui, F. Weng, H. Zhao, F. Ma, and Q. Tian (2026e)\n2610\tPairCoder: pair programming-inspired two-agent collaboration for code generation\n2611\t.\n2612\tIn\n2613\tFindings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026\n2614\t,\n2615\tM. Liakata, V. P. Moreira, J. Zhang, and D. Jurgens (Eds.)\n2616\t,\n2617\tSan Diego, California, United States\n2618\t,\n2619\tpp. 3043–3058\n2620\t.\n2621\tExternal Links:\n2622\tLink\n2623\t,\n2624\tDocument\n2625\t,\n2626\tISBN 979-8-89176-395-1\n2627\tCited by:\n2628\tText-Conditioned Symbolic Music Generation\n2629\t.\n26